{
  "title": "The Beast of the Volcano Caves Kept the Poorest Doors Warm | The Beast of Kanlaon",
  "description": "A work of fiction. All people and animals in this film are AI-generated. No real animals were harmed.<br><br>The rains have come to the slopes of Mount Kanlaon, yet the spring that feeds a barrio of five stilt huts is running dry. Someone is taking the water. The barrio has a different fear: the Amomongo, a long-nailed beast said to live in the steaming caves of the volcano, which they blame for every slip of mud on the slope.<br><br>Ramon Tabanao, a widowed water-carrier with a cough he cannot shake, keeps finding a warm black stone on his bamboo step on the coldest mornings. When he finally learns where the stones come from, it is a hot spring at a cave mouth high on the volcano, and the creature beside it is nothing like the stories: shy, silent, and quietly warming stones for the poorest doors in the barrio. It never asks for anything. It can dig, though. Its nails cut the trench, Ramon lays the split bamboo, and warm water runs down the mountain to the dry barrio again.<br><br>Up at the sugar plantation, the overseer Faustino Marquez has his own plans for the spring, and for the barrio&apos;s fear: a new ditch across the mountainside, land deeds bought cheap from frightened families, and a year&apos;s wages for anyone who can show him the creature&apos;s den. On the night of the search, fog swallows the mountain, one of his own men is left behind in the cold mud, and everyone holding a lantern has to choose between that reward and what they see with their own eyes.<br><br>The Beast of Kanlaon is an original cinematic story inspired by the Amomongo of Visayan folklore. Near La Castellana, at the foot of Mount Kanlaon in Negros Occidental, it is described as a shaggy creature with long nails that lives in the caves of the volcano, and villagers&apos; reports of it made Philippine news in 2008. Our Amomongo is a gentle retelling of that legend. The barrio, the plantation and every character in the film are invented.<br><br>Chapters<br><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vYFKZBuHo_0\">0:00</a> Prologue<br><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vYFKZBuHo_0&amp;t=58\">0:58</a> Chapter One: The Warm Stones<br><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vYFKZBuHo_0&amp;t=467\">7:47</a> Chapter Two: The Hot Spring<br><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vYFKZBuHo_0&amp;t=849\">14:09</a> Chapter Three: The Deeds<br><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vYFKZBuHo_0&amp;t=1286\">21:26</a> Chapter Four: The Fog<br><br>Written &amp; directed by Gilded Myth Pictures<br>Cast: Ramon Tabanao, a water-carrier · Celia Tabanao, his daughter · Faustino Marquez, the plantation overseer · Nilo Arcega, his timekeeper · Arsenio Reyes, the justice of the peace · The Amomongo, a creature of the volcano caves<br>Music: YouTube Audio Library<br><br><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/hashtag/mythology\">#mythology</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/hashtag/fantasyfilm\">#fantasyfilm</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/hashtag/legend\">#legend</a>",
